ghost-matter-48330f34.conceptual_definition:
The formula relates the minimum mass (
$M_{min}$
) required for a structure to form or remain stable on a given scale to the root-mean-square velocity (
$v_{rms}$
) of the constituent particles within that structure. The proportionality indicates that as the internal random motion of particles increases, a larger minimum mass is necessary to counteract the disruptive effects of this motion and maintain structural integrity.

local-unitary-520af705.limits_and_boundary:
This specific set of four operators represents discrete, fundamental transformations. In a broader sense, any single-qubit unitary operation can be constructed as a combination of rotations, which are continuous. However, these four operators are often chosen because they form a basis (e.g., the set 
$\{\mathbb{I}, \sigma_x, \sigma_y, \sigma_z\}
$ spans the space of Hermitian operators, and 
$\{\mathbb{I}, \sigma_x, \sigma_z, i\sigma_y\}
$ are a subset of unitary operations forming a group under composition). When considering larger quantum systems, 'local' unitary operations are those that act independently on individual qubits or subsystems, as opposed to 'global' operations that affect the entire system simultaneously.
